   "question": "What have MPs said about housing affordability and home ownership?",
   "answer": "MPs made opposing statements on housing affordability and home ownership.\n\nGovernment-aligned MPs argued that housing affordability was not in crisis, saying home ownership has \u201cnever been as achievable\u201d and pointing to low interest rates (6.55 per cent in June 2002) compared with 17 per cent under Labor, the First Home Owners Grant, job creation, low inflation, and a Productivity Commission inquiry. They said the rise in house prices benefited 70 per cent of households, that household debt was manageable because assets outweighed debts, and that the Commonwealth contributed more to housing agreements than the states.\n\nOpposition-aligned MPs argued there was a serious housing affordability crisis, noting house prices had risen sharply\u2014for example, a typical Melbourne house jumped from four years to seven years of average full-time earnings\u2014and that housing affordability had fallen to record lows. They said low- and middle-income families and young people were being locked out of home ownership, that the government was ignoring public housing and urban policy, and that more government action, including programs to help families save for deposits and urban renewal, was needed.",
