   "question": "What positions have been taken on negative gearing and property tax concessions?",
   "answer": "According to the context:\n\n- The government/Coalition side opposed abolishing negative gearing. It said: \u201cWe on this side of the House will not be abolishing negative gearing, because we believe that if you make a loss on an investment you should be able to set that loss against income, as you can under the normal principles of taxation law.\u201d\n- The opposition/Labor Party was inconsistent on negative gearing. It first indicated abolishing negative gearing was \u201cone option that could be contemplated,\u201d then later ruled out ending negative gearing. The member for Werriwa said on *Lateline* that negative gearing was \u201cpart of Labor's policy review\u201d and that \u201cany smart policy maker would be looking at it,\u201d but the next day Labor said it was not reviewing negative gearing.\n- On property tax concessions, the government said there is no GST on an established home, and that first home buyers buying an established home pay no GST and get a $7,000 grant. The government also introduced the First Home Owners Scheme and announced a Productivity Commission inquiry into housing market factors, including land release and taxation treatment.\n- The government criticised state Labor governments for imposing stamp duty, saying stamp duty on an average home had risen from $6,000 to $16,000 in Melbourne and Sydney.\n- Certain Labor figures were described as supporting property tax measures such as a capital gains tax on the family home and a \u201c30 per cent betterment tax,\u201d while also proposing alternatives like a progressive expenditure tax and regional GSTs.",
